theory.intro:
Once the sides are named from a chosen angle, each pair of sides gives a fraction. These three fractions are the sine, cosine and tangent ratios of that angle.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1]
\coordinate (P) at (0,0);
\coordinate (Q) at (4.2,0);
\coordinate (R) at (0,3.2);
\draw (P)--(Q) node[midway,below] {$A$};
\draw (Q)--(R) node[midway,above right] {$H$};
\draw (R)--(P) node[midway,left] {$O$};
\coordinate (ra1) at ($(P)!0.32cm!(Q)$);
\coordinate (ra2) at ($(P)!0.32cm!(R)$);
\draw (ra1)--($(ra1)+(ra2)-(P)$)--(ra2);
\pic[draw,"$\theta$",angle radius=0.62cm,angle eccentricity=1.75] {angle=R--Q--P};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

